507],{"class":413},[403,1427,1105],{"class":1104},[403,1429,1430],{"class":413}," })",[403,1432,1144],{"class":413},[403,1434,1436,1439,1442,1445,1447,1449,1451,1453,1455,1457,1460,1462,1464,1466,1469,1471,1473,1476,1478,1480,1482],{"class":405,"line":1435},21,[403,1437,1438],{"class":843},"    const",[403,1440,1441],{"class":417}," start",[403,1443,1444],{"class":413}," =",[403,1446,853],{"class":409},[403,1448,1095],{"class":503},[403,1450,484],{"class":409},[403,1452,1259],{"class":474},[403,1454,494],{"class":503},[403,1456,1374],{"class":413},[403,1458,1459],{"class":436},"\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Foidc\u002Fstart\u002F",[403,1461,1055],{"class":413},[403,1463,1416],{"class":417},[403,1465,286],{"class":413},[403,1467,1468],{"class":417},"id",[403,1470,1139],{"class":413},[403,1472,421],{"class":413},[403,1474,1475],{"class":413}," {}",[403,1477,1285],{"class":503},[403,1479,286],{"class":413},[403,1481,1398],{"class":474},[403,1483,478],{"class":503},[403,1485,1487,1490,1492,1495,1497,1500,1502,1505,1507,1510],{"class":405,"line":1486},22,[403,1488,1489],{"class":417},"    window",[403,1491,286],{"class":413},[403,1493,1494],{"class":417},"location",[403,1496,286],{"class":413},[403,1498,1499],{"class":474},"assign",[403,1501,494],{"class":503},[403,1503,1504],{"class":417},"start",[403,1506,286],{"class":413},[403,1508,1509],{"class":417},"redirect",[403,1511,552],{"class":503},[403,1513,1515],{"class":405,"line":1514},23,[403,1516,1331],{"class":413},[247,1518,1519,1520,1523,1524,1527,1528,1531],{},"The login page offers every method the application supports and lets the user choose: the password form, and a button for each configuration ",[276,1521,1522],{},"GET \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Fproviders"," lists as ",[276,1525,1526],{},"[{id, name, provider}]",", whether the organization's own provider or one of the system's shared Google and Microsoft registrations. No endpoint takes an email before the user has chosen a method, so a login page cannot be used to learn which emails have a user. An AI assistant building the page can ask the same question through the ",[276,1529,1530],{},"ApplicationService.getLoginProviders"," MCP tool.",[247,1533,1534,1535,1538,1539,1542,1543,1546,1547,1550],{},"A login belongs to the page that made it, and the API host accepts one only from a page that is one of the application's own UIs. ",[276,1536,1537],{},"GET \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fme"," answers ",[276,1540,1541],{},"204"," while the page is signed in, and ",[276,1544,1545],{},"POST \u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Flogout"," signs it out; both take ",[276,1548,1549],{},"credentials: 'include'"," as well.",[251,1552,1554],{"id":1553},"signing-in-to-a-tenant","Signing in to a tenant",[247,1556,1557,1558,1561,1562,1565,1566,1568,1569,1571,1572,1575,1576,1579,1580,1583,1584,1587,1588,1591],{},"An application whose tenants its administrators manage gives each tenant a login of its own. A page served at one of the tenant's sites, ",[276,1559,1560],{},"\u003CtenantId>--\u003CapplicationId>--\u003CuiName>"," under your organization's subdomain of the sites domain (see ",[281,1563,84],{"href":1564},"\u002Fapps\u002Fpersistence\u002Fmulti-tenancy#tenants-and-their-sites","), signs in to that tenant with the same calls as above: the API host reads the tenant from the page's host, so ",[276,1567,1371],{}," lists what the tenant's login offers — the tenant's own providers, then the application's — and ",[276,1570,1266],{}," admits only the tenant's users. A page that is not one of the tenant's sites, such as a UI running locally, names the tenant instead, with ",[276,1573,1574],{},"?tenantId="," on the ",[276,1577,1578],{},"GET"," and the start, and ",[276,1581,1582],{},"tenantId"," in the login body; the API host answers ",[276,1585,1586],{},"404"," for a tenant the application does not have, and ",[276,1589,1590],{},"403"," when a tenant's site names another tenant.",[251,1593,1595],{"id":1594},"how-oidc-works-with-your-application","How OIDC Works with Your Application",[1597,1598,1599,1621,1624,1627],"ol",{},[259,1600,1601,1602,1605,1606,1609,1610,1613,1614,1617,1618],{},"Your organization administrator creates one or more ",[276,1603,1604],{},"ApplicationOidcConfiguration","s for your application's login, or ",[276,1607,1608],{},"TenantOidcConfiguration","s for one tenant's, in the portal under the application's ",[262,1611,1612],{},"Settings → Sign-in providers"," tab, and registers the callback URL the tab shows for each, ",[276,1615,1616],{},"https:\u002F\u002F\u003CorganizationId>--\u003CapplicationId>.\u003Capp API domain>\u002Fapi\u002Fauth\u002Fapp\u002Flogin\u002Foidc\u002Fcallback\u002F\u003CconfigId>",", with the provider; or names one of the system's shared Google and Microsoft registrations in the application's ",[276,1619,1620],{},"socialProviders",[259,1622,1623],{},"Your UI renders a button per provider the login offers; the button posts the configuration's id to the start at your API host, whose answer carries the URL to send the browser to",[259,1625,1626],{},"The provider returns the browser to the API host, which validates the provider's response, looks the user up in your application, or in the tenant the sign-in is to, and sets the session cookie. A shared registration returns the browser to the app server's shared host instead, which validates the response and hands the verified identity to your application's API host under a single-use code, which completes only in the browser that started the sign-in; that host looks the user up and sets the cookie",[259,1628,1629,1630,1633,1634,1637,1638,1641,1642,1644,1645,1648,1649,1651,1652],{},"The browser returns to ",[276,1631,1632],{},"\u002F"," on the site that started the sign-in, which connects with the session; a sign-in that fails returns instead to the ",[276,1635,1636],{},"\u002Flogin"," page of that site with an ",[276,1639,1640],{},"error"," parameter, so every UI that offers sign-in serves a ",[276,1643,1636],{}," route. A failure that no longer knows which site started it — the flow's session expired, or a shared registration's single-use code was refused with ",[276,1646,1647],{},"handoff_invalid"," — returns to the ",[276,1650,1636],{}," page of your application's ",[281,1653,1655],{"href":1654},"\u002Fapps\u002Fapplication-structure\u002Fapplications-and-projects#applications","primary UI",[251,1657,1659],{"id":1658},"reusable-providers","Reusable Providers",[247,1661,1662,1663,1666,1667,1670,1671,1674,1675,1677,1678,1680,1681,1684,1685,1688,1689,1691,1692,1695],{},"Kinotic OS registers once with Google and Microsoft. A system user provisions these as ",[276,1664,1665],{},"ApplicationSocialOidcConfiguration"," entities, and your application offers one by naming its provider kind, ",[276,1668,1669],{},"google"," or ",[276,1672,1673],{},"azure-ad",", in its ",[276,1676,1620],{}," — no registration of your own with the provider. The registration is shared by every application that offers it, so a user who revokes it at the provider revokes it for all of them. Your organization's own providers for your application are ",[276,1679,1604],{}," entities your organization admin creates for it on the application's ",[262,1682,1683],{},"Sign-in providers"," settings tab, each registered with the provider under the application's own callback URL; the organization's own enterprise SSO for the portal is an ",[276,1686,1687],{},"OrganizationOidcConfiguration",", and an admin who wants the same provider at both copies it from one scope to the other. An ",[276,1690,1604],{}," whose ",[276,1693,1694],{},"createUserOnSignIn"," is on creates a user of your application from an identity it verifies on that identity's first sign-in, when the system manages your application's tenants; see below.",[247,1697,1698,1699,1701,1702,1704],{},"All are kinds of ",[276,1700,278],{}," — see ",[281,1703,167],{"href":168}," for how ownership is captured by the subtype.",[243,1706,1708],{"id":1707},"user-management","User Management",[247,1710,1711,1712,1715,1716,1718,1719,1721,1722,1725,1726,1729,1730,1732,1733,1735,1736,1739,1740,1742,1743,1746],{},"Application-scope users are ",[262,1713,1714],{},"invited"," by an administrator before they can authenticate, unless one of your application's own ",[276,1717,1604],{},"s says otherwise. Having a valid Google account never grants access to your application by itself: the system's shared registrations create no users. A configuration your organization registered for the application may have ",[276,1720,1694],{}," on, and then an identity that provider verifies which no user is bound to (an ",[276,1723,1724],{},"OidcBinding"," of the provider's configuration and ",[276,1727,1728],{},"sub"," to a user) becomes a user of the application on its first sign-in, with a tenant of its own, which is why it applies only while the system manages the application's tenants. Under tenant administrators a user is never without a tenant, so there a user is created by an administrator, an invitation, or a tenant's own ",[276,1731,1608],{}," with ",[276,1734,1694],{},", which makes the identity its provider verifies a user of that tenant on its first sign-in at the tenant's login; the application's own configurations never create a user into a tenant. An identity whose verified email the application already holds under another method is refused with ",[276,1737,1738],{},"user_exists",". A configuration of your own for Google with ",[276,1741,1694],{}," admits any Google account, which is sign-up with Google for your application. Self-service signup with a form applies to the ",[281,1744,1745],{"href":163},"Organization scope"," only.",[247,1748,1749,1750,1753,1754,1757,1758,1761,1762,1764],{},"Your organization administrator manages your application's users on the application's ",[262,1751,1752],{},"Users"," page in the portal. ",[262,1755,1756],{},"Invite user"," emails an invitation (",[276,1759,1760],{},"MemberService.inviteMember(email, displayName, applicationId)","); the invitee accepts it by choosing a password, which creates the application's user, and is then sent to your application's ",[281,1763,1655],{"href":1654},", where they sign in with that email and password from then on. The first UI one of your projects publishes becomes the primary UI unless you have chosen one; while the application has none, the accept page confirms the new user instead. An invitation into an application is accepted with a password only, and a user created this way has no OIDC identity bound to it, so it signs in with the password even when the email matches an identity at one of the application's providers.",[247,1766,1767,1768,1770],{},"For details on user and OIDC configuration management, see the ",[281,1769,162],{"href":163}," platform guide.",[1772,1773,1774],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .s7zQu, html code.shiki .s7zQu{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-light-font-style:italic;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-default-font-style:italic;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic}html pre.shiki code .sMK4o, html code.shiki .sMK4o{--shiki-light:#39ADB5;--shiki-default:#89DDFF;--shiki-dark:#89DDFF}html pre.shiki code .sTEyZ, html code.shiki .sTEyZ{--shiki-light:#90A4AE;--shiki-default:#EEFFFF;--shiki-dark:#BABED8}html pre.shiki code .sfazB, html code.shiki .sfazB{--shiki-light:#91B859;--shiki-default:#C3E88D;--shiki-dark:#C3E88D}html pre.shiki code .s2Zo4, html code.shiki .s2Zo4{--shiki-light:#6182B8;--shiki-default:#82AAFF;--shiki-dark:#82AAFF}html pre.shiki code .swJcz, html code.shiki .swJcz{--shiki-light:#E53935;--shiki-default:#F07178;--shiki-dark:#F07178}html .light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html.light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font